---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const site = Astro.site?.href ?? 'https://88cutouts.com/';

const jsonld = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'ContactPage',
    name: 'Contact 88cutouts',
    url: new URL('/contact/', site).href,
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: '88cutouts', url: site },
    mainEntity: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: '88cutouts',
      url: site,
      email: 'hello@88cutouts.com',
      contactPoint: {
        '@type': 'ContactPoint',
        contactType: 'customer support',
        email: 'hello@88cutouts.com',
        availableLanguage: 'English',
      },
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Contact — requests, licensing and takedowns | 88cutouts"
  description="Get in touch with 88cutouts: request a sticker, ask about commercial use, report a problem or request a takedown. One person reads everything."
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Contact</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        One address, read by one person:
        <a href="mailto:hello@88cutouts.com">hello@88cutouts.com</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is no contact form, no ticket system and no chat widget — 88cutouts is run by
        one person, and email is the honest version of what that means. Replies usually come
        within a few days.
      </p>

      <h2>Requesting a sticker</h2>
      <p>
        This is the most useful thing you can email about. If there is a cutout you keep
        looking for and cannot find — a shape, an object, a phrase, a colour that the
        library is missing — say so. Requests genuinely drive what gets drawn next, and a
        specific description ("a green gingham bow", "a cassette in pink") is far easier to
        act on than a general one.
      </p>

      <h2>Commercial use and licensing</h2>
      <p>
        Everything here is free for personal use, and reselling or repackaging the files is
        not allowed. Anything in between — a small business using a cutout on a Story, a
        print run, a client project — needs asking about, and the answer is often yes. Tell
        me which file and what for. The full terms are on the
        <a href="/about/">about page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting a problem</h2>
      <p>
        Broken image, a sticker that pastes with a halo, a page that will not load, a link
        that goes nowhere: worth reporting, and quick to fix. Including the page URL and
        which browser you were using saves a round trip.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same goes for the guides on the <a href="/blog/">blog</a>. If something in one
        of them is wrong or out of date — app interfaces change constantly — it gets
        corrected rather than argued about.
      </p>

      <h2>Takedown requests</h2>
      <p>
        Everything on this site is made for it. If you believe something here infringes your
        work, email with a link to the page and to your original, and the file comes down
        while I look into it rather than after. That is the whole process, and it does not
        need a lawyer to start.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy questions</h2>
      <p>
        There are no accounts here and nothing you do on the site is uploaded, so there is
        rarely any personal data to ask about — but if you have a question about analytics,
        cookies or advertising, the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> covers it and the
        same address answers anything it does not.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
